!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/40/io/ltr8/http/deployment.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/40/m/policy.tn"
@doc:"""
  A deployment descriptor: how ONE INSTANCE is configured, as against what a document must be (a schema)
  or what an endpoint offers (an API description).

  [TSON-DATA] §8.2 is a security control -- a restriction level and an optional script set over values, and
  over names a unit and a look-alike switch as well, enforced by default -- and it says no document may name,
  import or otherwise select the policy it is judged under. This is where a deployment states that policy, in
  the vocabulary the bundled policy.tn declares, imported here. The short form of the argument is that the two
  obvious homes are both wrong, and for reasons stronger than the decision being orthogonal:

  A SCHEMA cannot hold it. An artifact that declared its own strictness would choose its own check, and a
  document carrying homographs would simply declare the level that admits them. §3.5 also makes a published
  schema immutable and §2.2.1 lets it be hash-pinned, while a policy must move -- confusables.txt updates,
  threat models change -- so raising a level would mint a new identity and every document pinning the old one
  would keep the old policy for good. [TSON-DATA] §8.3 settles it independently: skeleton distinctness does
  not compose across !!import, so the policy is a property of the merged namespace rather than of any one
  schema.

  An API DESCRIPTION cannot hold it either, being a schema here and inheriting both, and it would put policy
  in a contract -- raising a token policy would mean publishing a new description, which is the friction that
  gets a control switched off.

  A DESCRIPTOR IS DATA, NOT A SCHEMA -- this document, the vocabulary a descriptor is written in, is the
  schema -- and the line is worth stating. A document that exports a namespace is a schema, and any document
  may refer to a type or a value in the namespace it imports. An API description is a schema because others
  refer into it. A descriptor configures one instance, may move without minting an identity, and exports
  nothing, since nothing may name it (rule 2 below), so it need not be a schema.

  Two rules hold, or self-certification returns by the back door:

    1. A processor is HANDED a descriptor; it does not go looking for one. A descriptor is diffable and
       reviewable where an environment variable is not, but a runtime that loads whatever is on its path
       still lets a container image change a security policy with no code diff.
    2. NO DOCUMENT MAY NAME ONE. There is no !!import of a descriptor and no reference to one from a schema
       or a data document. The moment a document can point at a descriptor, it picks its own enforcement.

  Rule 2 is why `deployment.acceptance_profile` below is a separate type rather than this one served as-is:
  what a counterparty may see is a PROJECTION, derived from what the processor enforces, never the descriptor.

  The policy a descriptor states is policy.tn's `policy.processor`, COMPLETE, and applied exactly as written: what is
  in the file is what runs. There are no partial overrides falling back to the library's defaults, because a
  default is the library's to move, and a security setting whose effect can shift under a library upgrade
  with no diff to the descriptor is the silent relaxation §8.2 exists to prevent. A descriptor that states
  no policy leaves the library's defaults alone; one that states a policy states every required member of
  `policy.processor`, and the one optional member, `unicode_data_version`, is the processor's (below).

  ONE ROOT NAME. Every entry is a member of the schema map `deployment` nested in the root, and is named by
  its path from the root, from inside the map as from outside it: `deployment.descriptor`,
  `deployment.listener`, `deployment.acceptance_profile`. Every root name of an import closure is reserved in
  each importer ([TSON-SCHEMA] §2.2.3), so a vocabulary declaring `descriptor` or `listener` at its root would
  forbid an importer those names. Nested in one map, it reserves `deployment` only against a root that is no
  schema map: a nested schema map is open, so another schema's `deployment => !schema { … }` adds its members
  to this one, and only a path both declare collides. A descriptor is a data document importing this schema
  whose root is `!deployment.descriptor { … }`.
  """
!schema {
  deployment => !schema {
    @doc:"""
      Where this instance listens. Absent members leave the caller's own defaults alone.

      `host` is net.tn's: a host name or an IPv4 or IPv6 address, one string-class type, so `localhost`,
      `"127.0.0.1"`, `"::1"` and `"[::1]"` are written untagged, and an address compares as an address.
      """
    listener => {
      host?: host
      port?: uint16
    }

    @doc:"""
      One deployment: the descriptor a processor is handed.

      `policy` is the processor policy this deployment runs under, a `policy.processor` carried whole: its
      identifier and token policies and its `limits`, every one stated, and its `unicode_data_version`, each a
      member of that record rather than a field of this one. Absent leaves the library's defaults, which point
      opposite ways round for reasons §8.2 gives -- a name is an interface and a homograph in one is an attack,
      while data may legitimately be a Cyrillic display name -- so a descriptor stating none is asking for
      exactly that, not for nothing. `unicode_data_version` is the one optional member of `policy.processor`,
      and a descriptor leaves it out or writes the processor's own: the tables are the processor's, not the
      deployment's, and `TsonDeployment` refuses a descriptor naming a version this processor does not carry.

      Scripts are named by their [UAX #24] Script property alias, long (`Latin`, `Canadian_Aboriginal`) or ISO
      15924 (`Latn`, `Cans`), case-insensitively; a name that is not a script stops the read, so a typo in a
      security setting halts the process rather than being carried as a policy quietly missing a script.

      `schema_hosts` is the fetch allow-list: which schema identities this deployment will load over the
      network, each a net.tn `host` -- a host name in either label form (`bücher.example` is
      `xn--bcher-kva.example`, and `Example.COM` is `example.com`), or an address. An entry is compared by the
      type's own equality, so the spelling an operator writes does not decide what matches, and a URL, a port or
      a path is refused when the descriptor is read. Empty means none, which is a real posture rather than a
      missing setting -- a service that resolves only what it was given fetches nothing. It is deliberately NOT
      in `deployment.acceptance_profile`: a counterparty has no business knowing which origins this deployment trusts.
      """
    descriptor => {
      name:          text
      listener?:     deployment.listener
      policy?:       policy.processor
      schema_hosts?: set<host>
    }

    @doc:"""
      What a counterparty may see: the answer to "what will this endpoint accept?", and nothing else.

      Served at a well-known path rather than at an identity's, and the reason is the same one that makes a
      descriptor unnameable. In this series everything with an identity is served at its identity's path
      (§2.2.1); a descriptor is precisely the artifact that must NOT have an identity, so a well-known path is
      right for it for the same reason it would be wrong for a schema.

      `policy` is the policy IN FORCE, in policy.tn's shape -- the shape `tson policy` and the CLI's report
      state one in. It is derived from the processor rather than copied from the descriptor, so a descriptor
      stating none still publishes the library's defaults, and the fetch allow-list is internal topology, so it
      cannot drift into it.

      A PROFILE IS A HINT AND THE VERDICT IS THE AUTHORITY. This can be cached, and a policy can change under
      it; only the 4xx a request actually receives says what applied to that request. The refusal itself
      carries only its code: §8.2 has the policy and the data version stated once per processor rather than on
      every refusal, and this profile is that statement for a counterparty. `unicode_data_version` and `limits`
      are members of `policy.processor`, which the profile carries whole. The version is always written here,
      as a report writes it: §8.3 marks all three rules unstable across Unicode releases, so a refusal is only
      interpretable against the data that produced it. The limits are here for the same reason the policies
      are: a 413 says a document went past a bound, and a sender that read the bound first never writes past
      it.
      """
    acceptance_profile => {
      name:   text
      policy: policy.processor
    }
  }
}
